  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Rapid escalation in weapons deliveries raises counterparty and settlement risks (and can trigger secondary sanctions), causing primary dealers and market-makers to reduce inventories and repo/intermediation activity, thereby degrading bond market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_rate_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::weapons_transfer_rate" target="iran_regional_conflict::conflict_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in outside weapons deliveries into the Ukraine theatre push Russia to source arms (notably UAVs and munitions) from Iran and accelerate Iran's production/exports, enabling wider distribution of Iranian systems to regional proxies and raising regional fighting levels.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::weapons_transfer_rate" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::supply_chain_resilience" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A surge in weapons and electronics transfers diverts scarce components, testing capacity and logistics into military channels, producing immediate shortages and degradation in the commercial semiconductor supply chain's ability to absorb and recover from shocks relevant to the US–China rivalry.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
